  "protocol": {
    "form": "every variant keeps the chance-corrected shape 1 - sum(W*O)/sum(W*E), so 0 is chance for each",
    "official": "W[i,j] = (i-j)^2 * |i-2|^2  (src/data.py WEIGHT, the graded metric)",
    "flat_credit": "same as official but true-flat rows get (i-j)^2 instead of 0, so correctly answering flat earns credit",
    "linear": "W[i,j] = |i-j| * |i-2|^2, so being far wrong costs less steeply",
    "plain_qwk": "W[i,j] = (i-j)^2, no extra weight on extreme truth rows",
    "extreme_only": "W[i,j] = (i-j)^2 where |i-2| == 2, else 0, so only crash and spike days count"
  },
  "limitation": "The non-B0 candidates had their internal knobs (scale, delta, tau, thresholds) chosen under the official score, so this measures how already-frozen predictions rank under a different ruler, not how each model would look if it had been tuned for that ruler.",
  "decision_rule": "This is descriptive. Nothing is adopted whatever the result: the holdout was opened on 2026-10-09, B0 won, and the submission is frozen. A variant where B0 loses is reported as a caveat on the conclusion, never as grounds to change the submission.",
  "reading": "Two things matter more than the ranks. First, every rule-family candidate sits inside a band narrower than 0.002 under each ruler, so B0 moving between rank 3 and rank 8 is noise, not a change of winner - and B0 was never the development mean winner anyway; the pre-registered criteria, not the mean, are what kept it. Second, B0 beats the best tree model by 0.021 under the official score but by only 0.002 under plain_qwk, so the gap rule owes its edge to the extreme-truth weighting in the official metric rather than to being a better forecast - lgb_cost is far more accurate (0.378 against 0.262) under any ruler."
